WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.858
Every second, the amount of electricity generated must match the amount consumed.

2
00:00:05.888 --> 00:00:07.939
But forecasts are never perfect.

3
00:00:07.969 --> 00:00:11.450
A cloud passes, a plant trips, a factory starts early.

4
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.182
Someone must correct the gap in real time.

5
00:00:14.787 --> 00:00:17.480
In this episode, we look at balancing markets,

6
00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.966
and how the cost of deviations is settled.

7
00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.818
In Europe, every producer,

8
00:00:23.848 --> 00:00:27.511
supplier and trader belongs to a balance responsible party.

9
00:00:27.541 --> 00:00:32.520
It submits a schedule to the grid operator for each period, based on its trades,

10
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.965
and it is financially responsible for any deviation from that schedule.

11
00:00:38.053 --> 00:00:38.362
Here,

12
00:00:38.392 --> 00:00:43.783
wind portfolio A scheduled one hundred megawatt-hours but produced only seventy,

13
00:00:43.813 --> 00:00:45.476
so it is short by thirty.

14
00:00:45.506 --> 00:00:50.490
Portfolio B scheduled fifty but produced seventy, so it is long by twenty.

15
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:57.882
The transmission system operator watches the total imbalance of the whole system.

16
00:00:57.912 --> 00:01:00.877
Here the system is short by ten megawatt-hours:

17
00:01:00.907 --> 00:01:03.426
thirty missing, minus twenty in surplus.

18
00:01:03.456 --> 00:01:10.181
So the operator activates upward balancing energy from providers who agreed to raise output or cut demand.

19
00:01:10.783 --> 00:01:15.940
If the system were long instead, the operator would activate downward balancing,

20
00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:19.182
asking providers to reduce output or consume more.

21
00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:22.229
These reserves react within seconds to minutes,

22
00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:24.822
and the next episode explains each type.

23
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.251
Now the settlement.

24
00:01:28.281 --> 00:01:32.929
Balancing has a cost, and it is charged to those who caused the imbalance.

25
00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:34.636
For each settlement period,

26
00:01:34.666 --> 00:01:40.262
the operator calculates an imbalance price from the cost of the balancing energy it used.

27
00:01:40.292 --> 00:01:43.802
Suppose it is one hundred fifty euros per megawatt-hour.

28
00:01:44.413 --> 00:01:48.836
Portfolio A is short thirty, so it pays thirty times one hundred fifty:

29
00:01:48.866 --> 00:01:50.905
four thousand five hundred euros.

30
00:01:50.935 --> 00:01:52.598
Portfolio B is long twenty,

31
00:01:52.628 --> 00:01:56.800
and under a single price it is paid twenty times one hundred fifty:

32
00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:00.562
three thousand euros, because its surplus helped the system.

33
00:02:00.592 --> 00:02:05.077
The net amount, fifteen hundred euros, helps cover the operator's costs.

34
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.450
All numbers are illustrative.

35
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.367
Imbalance prices can be far from market prices,

36
00:02:10.397 --> 00:02:16.325
which is why participants try to correct their positions in the intraday market before delivery.

37
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.102
Imbalances are settled in short periods.

38
00:02:21.132 --> 00:02:26.462
The European balancing rules set the imbalance settlement period at fifteen minutes,

39
00:02:26.492 --> 00:02:29.525
so each hour has four separate imbalance prices.

40
00:02:29.555 --> 00:02:33.544
A participant can be short in one period, and long in the next.

41
00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:39.074
In Serbia, the transmission system operator, EMS,

42
00:02:39.104 --> 00:02:43.645
is responsible for balancing the system and for settling imbalances.

43
00:02:43.675 --> 00:02:47.679
Serbia belongs to the Continental European synchronous area,

44
00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.713
and its rules continue to be aligned with European practice,

45
00:02:51.743 --> 00:02:54.670
so always check the latest EMS publications.

46
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:57.991
Three takeaways.

47
00:02:58.021 --> 00:02:58.262
One:

48
00:02:58.292 --> 00:03:01.911
every participant is responsible for its own schedule.

49
00:03:01.941 --> 00:03:02.181
Two:

50
00:03:02.211 --> 00:03:05.357
the operator balances the system with reserves,

51
00:03:05.387 --> 00:03:08.668
and the cost is charged through imbalance prices.

52
00:03:08.698 --> 00:03:09.074
Three:

53
00:03:09.104 --> 00:03:14.480
correcting positions early, in the intraday market, helps avoid imbalance costs.

54
00:03:15.073 --> 00:03:19.316
Next, we look at the reserves that keep the grid stable:

55
00:03:19.346 --> 00:03:20.765
FCR, aFRR and mFRR.

56
00:03:20.795 --> 00:03:23.359
Thanks for watching Power Academy.
